(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-medical-literature-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Medical Literature Master Exam — Form C","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is random assignment preferred when testing a treatment?","choices":["It limits systematic differences between groups","It removes the need for a comparison group","It ensures every participant complies","It keeps participants unaware of their group"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: cohort comparisons are limited by systematic differences in groups other than the treatment factor, which random assignment is used to limit. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How is epidemiology defined?","choices":["The study of disease mechanisms at the cellular and molecular level","The statistical analysis of data collected during a single clinical trial","The distribution and determinants of health-related states in populations","The study of how drugs are absorbed, distributed and eliminated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the definition does not stop at studying: it extends to applying that study to the control of health problems, which is what makes it a practical discipline rather than a descriptive one. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A trade group's guideline endorses only products sold by its member companies. Which conflict of interest is this?","choices":["Intellectual conflict of interest","Financial conflict of interest","Personal conflict of interest","Institutional conflict"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because institutional conflict arises when a recommendation benefits one organization more than others. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How should the comparison group in a cohort study be chosen?","choices":["At random from the general population","To match the exposed group on outcome","As similar as possible except for the exposure","To be as different as possible from the exposed group"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because any other difference between the groups becomes an alternative explanation for whatever difference in outcome appears. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is susceptibility bias?","choices":["One group's outcomes are more likely to be detected","The assembled groups differ in ways that affect outcome","Patients drop out or change groups","Results apply only to a narrow population"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: for example the groups may be at different points in the disease course, have other concurrent disease, or have had prior treatment. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"In a medication trial, the placebo control group turns out to be older on average than the experimental group. What does this represent?","choices":["Measurement bias in recording age","Attrition effect","Age acting as a confounding variable","Reporting bias by the investigators"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because age is plausibly associated with both group membership and the outcome, so its effect cannot be separated from the effect of the medication being tested. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A patient's blood pressure differs between two readings taken at different times of day. Which variation does that illustrate?","choices":["Random measurement error","True biologic variation from unknown random factors","Interobserver variation","Systematic variation, since the condition is known to affect it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: time of day is a known condition of measurement affecting the value, alongside position, smoking beforehand and the white coat phenomenon. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Why is mortality listed as a surveillance strategy?","choices":["Mortality rates are the most accurate measure of disease","Mortality determines the case definition","Some cases are detected only at death","Deaths are easier to count than cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which makes death certification a genuine source of case finding rather than merely a record of outcome. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What must a cohort study have so that attrition does not falsely skew the outcome?","choices":["A larger control group","Recall of past exposures","Random assignment to exposure","Complete follow-up of the cohort"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: if participants drop out, the outcome data can be falsely skewed, so the cohort must also be followed long enough to observe the expected outcome. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which objective fits a program that screens for hypertension?","choices":["Change a behavioral risk factor","Find disease so treatment can cure it","Change a metabolic risk factor through diet","Find disease so treatment prevents complications"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treating hypertension prevents its complications, unlike screening for conditions where treatment can cure. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the interquartile range?","choices":["The variability of scores about the mean","The full spread from lowest to highest value","A limited range index, such as the 25th to 75th percentiles","The proportion of values within one standard deviation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and limiting it that way is the point: it describes the spread of the bulk of the data without being dictated by the two extreme values. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What must participants give to take part in a trial?","choices":["Proof they can comply with instructions","Knowledge of which group they are in","Consent to take part","A promise not to withdraw"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: participants must give consent, which is one of the conditions for an ethical trial alongside genuine uncertainty about the treatments. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which criterion in the modified Centor score can score minus one?","choices":["Absence of cough","Age over 45","Tonsillar swelling or exudate","Temperature above 38 degrees"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: ages 3 to 14 score one point, 15 to 44 score nothing, and over 45 scores minus one, because the infection becomes less likely with age. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which study limitation can produce a type II error?","choices":["A p value smaller than 0.001","Short follow-up or poor compliance","Blinding the investigators","Random assignment of participants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: too brief a follow-up or subjects not adhering can hide a real difference, so the result looks not significant though it is a false negative. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does the Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report carry?","choices":["Original randomized controlled trial reports","Systematic reviews of therapeutic questions","Individual state disease registry data","Public health policies for prevention and treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it sits among the guideline sources alongside the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality and the Task Force on Community Preventive Services. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What does a case-control study not need to do that a cohort study does?","choices":["Ask about earlier exposure","Define who counts as a case","Identify a comparison group","Wait for the condition to develop"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since the cases already have the condition, which is why results do not take years to obtain. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A trial compares surgical with medical therapy. Patients in the surgical arm were healthy enough to tolerate an operation, while the medical arm carried more comorbidities. Which bias does this illustrate?","choices":["Non-response bias","Publication bias","Selection bias","Measurement bias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The groups differ in baseline health as well as in treatment, so a difference in outcome cannot be attributed to the therapy, which is the defining problem of biased sampling. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"For a question comparing two diagnostic tests, which outcome fits?","choices":["Number of tests ordered","Sensitivity and specificity","Length of the clinic visit","Patient satisfaction with the result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because a question about how well a test detects disease can name the accuracy of each test, expressed as sensitivity and specificity, as its outcome. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How should a screening campaign combine tests?","choices":["Screen with a highly sensitive test, confirm with a highly specific one","Screen with a highly specific test, confirm with a sensitive one","Use two tests of equal sensitivity","Use the gold standard for screening"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the first pass should miss as few cases as possible and the second should avoid committing healthy people to further investigation. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does survival analysis calculate when people enter at various times?","choices":["The random error of each measurement","The sensitivity of the outcome measure","The prevalence at a single moment","The person-years each person contributes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: people can be added at different points in their disease and each contributes the time they are followed. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is study design?","choices":["The organization and plan for data collection and analysis","The number of participants enrolled","The clinical question the study sets out to answer","The statistical tests applied once data exist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it matters because it bears directly on the validity and reliability of the data: strong design gives strong data, and strong data gives valid conclusions. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which considerations apply when ordering a diagnostic test?","choices":["Repeat every abnormal test at least twice","Risk against benefit, the test's limits, and its cost","Order the most sensitive test available in every case","Order every test the patient requests"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside two instructions: avoid ordering tests indiscriminately, and avoid repeating a test without an indication. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How does prevalence help in clinical reasoning?","choices":["It identifies which test is most accurate","It prioritizes the differential diagnosis","It establishes the cause of the condition","It determines the severity of the condition"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: think horses, not zebras. It also affects the interpretation of test results, because it sets the pretest probability. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What marks a quality source of medical information?","choices":["Publication within the last twelve months","A large number of citations to the article","Endorsement by a pharmaceutical company","Peer review, with government databases a further category"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the matching warning is blunt: be cautious of websites, which carry no equivalent guarantee of scrutiny before publication. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"To which kinds of questions is grading of evidence most often applied?","choices":["Treatment and screening","Anatomy and staging","Prevalence and incidence","Etiology and disease naming"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and grading is a quick way to assess clinical significance, although it still involves some value judgment in weighing pros and cons. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does the crude mortality rate measure?","choices":["Deaths among those diagnosed with a disease","Deaths from one specified cause in a population","Deaths from all causes in a population","Deaths within a particular age group"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is called crude: it makes no adjustment for the age structure of the population, and an older population will show a higher figure for that reason alone. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What evidence should exist before screening is introduced?","choices":["That screening alters the natural history of disease","That most people would accept screening","That screened people live longer after diagnosis","That the test detects any abnormality"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classic statement asks for conclusive evidence that screening changes the natural history in a significant proportion of those screened. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Why is the age-adjusted death rate the right measure for comparing a county with its state?","choices":["It counts only deaths among residents","It uses live births as the denominator","It removes differences in age structure between the two","It expresses each cause as a percentage of deaths"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and without it a county with an older population would appear less healthy on the crude rate for that reason alone. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What kind of design is a randomized controlled trial?","choices":["Experimental, for testing hypotheses","Descriptive, for characterizing a single group","Observational, for testing hypotheses","Observational, for generating hypotheses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the investigator assigns and manipulates the intervention rather than observing an exposure that arose on its own. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which is a component of the burden of suffering from a condition?","choices":["Test sensitivity","Test simplicity","Disability","Treatment cost-effectiveness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Death, disease, disability, discomfort, dissatisfaction and destitution are the components of burden, together with measures such as hospitalizations and incidence. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why must an investigation confirm that an epidemic truly exists?","choices":["An apparent rise may reflect detection rather than occurrence","Epidemics are always reported by clinicians first","Endemic disease cannot produce clusters of cases","Attack rates cannot be determined otherwise"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why a baseline and a settled case definition come first: both awareness and definition change how many cases are found. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does five-year survival measure?","choices":["Percent of patients whose disease returns within five years","Percent of patients alive five years from a point in their disease","Percent of a population dying of the disease in five years","Percent of patients cured after five years of treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the starting point is some point in the course of the disease, which is why where that point falls matters. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which clinical question does the term Diagnosis address?","choices":["How does treatment change the course of the disease?","Does early detection improve the course of the disease?","What conditions lead to the disease?","How accurate are the tests used to identify the disease?"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The question is about the performance of the tests themselves, which is why test properties are studied as a topic in their own right rather than assumed. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which designs are observational?","choices":["Cohort and case-control","Meta-analyses","Quasi-experimental studies","Randomized controlled trials"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because in both the investigator watches an exposure that occurred independently rather than allocating it. The randomized trial is the experimental example. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How is strength of association judged in a causal assessment?","choices":["By whether a mechanism is known","By whether exposure can be reversed","By the size of the relative risk","By how many studies report it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a large relative risk is a strong association, which makes a causal relationship more likely. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How can the natural history of a disease be changed to improve prognosis?","choices":["Through longer observation alone","Through more precise measurement","Through larger study cohorts","Through clinical intervention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the further aim is to detect disease at an earlier point so that treatment is as effective as it can be. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What do prognostic factors identify?","choices":["Patients with the same disease but different prognoses","Patients with different diseases and equal prognoses","Healthy people who will develop the disease","Tests that confirm a diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is what makes them useful: they separate patients who share a diagnosis into groups that fare differently. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which conditions does screening tend to detect, giving rise to length-time bias?","choices":["Conditions that are never diagnosed","Conditions with no prognostic difference","Slow, favorable-prognosis conditions","Rapid conditions with poor prognoses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Slow conditions are present for longer, so a screen is likely to catch them, and they carry better prognoses than fast ones. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In a trial, 12 of 200 patients on a new drug and 30 of 200 on placebo had the outcome. What is the relative risk on the new drug?","choices":["0.15","0.4","2.5","0.06"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the risk on the new drug is 12/200 = 0.06 and on placebo 30/200 = 0.15, and 0.06 divided by 0.15 is 0.4. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Below what p value is a result generally accepted as statistically significant?","choices":["0.05","0.10","0.01","0.50"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, meaning a chance of about 1 in 20; a p value of 0.01 (1 in 100) is generally called highly significant and 0.001 (1 in 1000) very highly significant. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why are higher-level studies more likely to show that an outcome is due to what was studied?","choices":["They are least vulnerable to bias","They enroll patients from a single center","They report larger effect sizes","They are published in higher-impact journals"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Systematic error is what allows something other than the exposure or intervention to produce the observed outcome, so reducing it strengthens the attribution. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does the validity step of evidence-based practice assess?","choices":["Whether the outcome improved after the change","Whether the question was correctly framed","Whether the research applies to this patient","Whether the research is true"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it comes before application rather than after: research whose truth has not been established should not be carried into a patient encounter. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does statistical abnormality not necessarily imply?","choices":["That the value lies outside the reference range","Practical or clinical abnormality","That the value was measured accurately","That the test was performed correctly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with a serum cholesterol of 201 as the example: above the desired level, but not clinically significant in itself. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What does reliability mean?","choices":["The measurement is free of systematic error","The measurement captures what it is meant to","The result applies to other populations","Repeated attempts produce similar results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it goes by several other names: reproducibility, repeatability and precision. Note that none of them implies the result is correct. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which two situations both earn a Grade B?","choices":["High certainty of substantial benefit only","High certainty of moderate benefit, or moderate certainty of more","Insufficient evidence to weigh benefits against harms","Moderate certainty of a small benefit only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and either way the instruction is the same as for Grade A: offer or provide the service. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A test has a positive likelihood ratio of 4. What does that mean?","choices":["Four percent of positive results are correct","A positive result makes it four times more likely","The test is correct four times out of five","Four patients in every hundred have the disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, relative to the pre-test probability, which is what makes the ratio a multiplier rather than a probability in its own right. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which method gives each patient an equal chance of falling into either group?","choices":["Stratification","Matching","Restriction","Randomization"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: it is applied in the design phase and spreads confounders between the groups by chance. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which factors does effectiveness take into account?","choices":["Compliance, practicality and cost","Dose, half-life and mechanism of action","Randomization, blinding and allocation","Age, sex and diagnostic category"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: these real-world factors are what separate effectiveness, in ordinary circumstances, from efficacy, in ideal circumstances. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What distinguishes a cross-sectional ecologic study?","choices":["It uses population rather than individual level data","It measures each participant twice","It follows individuals forward over many years","It assigns an exposure to half the population"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, relating the frequency of a characteristic to an outcome of interest across populations rather than within individuals. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which three inputs combine to produce good clinical decision making?","choices":["External evidence, statistical significance and journal reputation","Clinical expertise, institutional policy and cost containment","Patient values, provider confidence and peer consensus","Clinical expertise, external evidence, and patient values"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the output named is not merely a decision but improved patient care, which is the point of combining all three rather than relying on any one. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What makes observational treatment studies hard to interpret?","choices":["Confounding and effect modification","The absence of any real-world patients","Use of blinding that hides the exposure","Excessive control over who receives treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: both are difficult to avoid when treatment is not assigned, so differences may reflect other factors rather than the treatment. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In a cohort 2x2 table with exposed cases (a), exposed noncases (b), unexposed cases (c) and unexposed noncases (d), which expression gives the relative risk?","choices":["ad divided by bc","a/(a+b) divided by c/(c+d)","a/(a+c) divided by b/(b+d)","a/c divided by b/d"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the incidence in the exposed, a over a plus b, divided by the incidence in the unexposed, c over c plus d. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why does random assignment strengthen causal conclusions?","choices":["It guarantees a significant result","It makes measurements more reliable","It balances confounders between the groups","It removes all random error from results"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: random assignment spreads confounding factors evenly, although random error remains and is still accounted for through p values. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which is an example of ordinal data?","choices":["Blood group","Cancer staging from I to IV","Number of heart attacks","Blood pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside pitting edema graded one to four plus, patient status or condition, and the drug risk categories for birth defects. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A study makes 60 comparisons between groups, none reflecting a true difference. If significance is set at a p value of 0.05, about how many will appear significant by chance?","choices":["6","3","0","30"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a p value of 0.05 corresponds to a chance of 1 in 20, and 60 divided by 20 is 3. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does parallel testing generally increase?","choices":["The prevalence of the disease","The reliability of each individual test","Specificity and positive predictive value","Sensitivity and negative predictive value"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so disease is less likely to be missed. How much sensitivity improves depends on how well each test catches the cases the other misses. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which topic suits a narrative review better than a systematic review?","choices":["An overview of many aspects of a disease","Whether one drug lowers mortality more than another","Whether screening lowers deaths in one age group","Whether a new test detects a disease more accurately"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because narrative reviews suit broad topics and a range of issues, whereas a systematic review needs a focused question. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"In an individual patient, what is the best predictor of disease?","choices":["Presence of a single risk factor","The relative risk of the exposure","Presence of early clinical disease","Incidence in the wider population"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: risk factors raise the probability of disease, but in an individual it is early clinical disease itself that predicts best. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"The number needed to harm for a side effect is 200. If 1,000 patients receive the intervention, how many are expected to be harmed?","choices":["200,000 patients","200 patients","5 patients","0.2 patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The number treated divided by the number needed to harm is 1,000 divided by 200, which is 5 patients harmed. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A reader asks how the studies in a systematic review were located. Which question belongs with it?","choices":["Were the tables printed in color?","Which inclusion and exclusion criteria were used?","How many pages does the reference list run?","Which reviewer wrote the discussion section?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because how studies were found and which criteria decided their entry are read together with how valid the original studies were. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"}]};
